Yes — with the same caution you already named: that is one deep layer of the economy, not the whole economy.

At bedrock, an economy is partly a giant distributed system for turning intention into useful consequence:

\text{knowledge} + \text{labor} + \text{tools} + \text{coordination}
\rightarrow
\text{goods/services}
\rightarrow
\text{exchange}

So when an economy struggles, one useful question is: where is the thought-to-action pipeline breaking?

Sometimes people cannot work. Sometimes they can work but lack tools, capital, training, transportation, childcare, health, or time. Sometimes they can produce something valuable but cannot find the person who values it. Sometimes companies know exactly what should be done but bureaucracy makes execution expensive. Sometimes millions of individually sensible actions fail to coordinate. Sometimes enormous productive capacity exists but demand, credit, trust, or infrastructure collapses.

That gives you several different “execution losses”:

\text{potential output}
-
\text{realized output}
=
\text{friction}

where friction can be physical, informational, institutional, financial, psychological, technological, or organizational.

And scale changes the problem. One person making a chair is largely a skill-and-materials problem. Producing ten million chairs requires supply chains, standards, financing, factories, logistics, contracts, energy, management, software, and trust. The work itself has not disappeared; it has been wrapped inside coordination machinery.

That is why some technologies have such enormous economic effects. A forklift does not merely make one worker stronger. Software does not merely make one clerk faster. They alter how much intention can be converted into consequence per unit of human attention.

You could almost define one dimension of economic development as:

\boxed{
\text{Execution leverage}
=
\frac{\text{useful consequences produced}}
{\text{human effort + time + coordination cost}}
}

Then automation, specialization, markets, corporations, money, contracts, roads, education, AI, and even language itself become different technologies for raising execution leverage.

But there is another important side: useful to whom?

An economy can become extraordinarily good at executing something that people do not actually need, or bad at producing things that are socially valuable but difficult to monetize. Price is a signaling mechanism, not a perfect measurement of human usefulness.

So I think your idea becomes stronger stated this way:

An economy is partly the visible wake left by a civilization’s capacity to turn distributed human intention into coordinated action.

GDP, employment, prices, firms, wages, trade, shortages — those are downstream traces.

Which loops directly back to your earlier principle: don’t mistake the dashboard for the machine underneath it.